'''anacosta''' (an-a-kos′tä̤), ''n.'' [Sp.] A woolen fabric made in Holland and exported to Spain.

'''Anacreontic''' (an-ak-rē-on′tik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''Anacreonticus'', < ''Anacreon'', < Gr. Ἀνακρέων, a Greek poet.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Pertaining to or after the manner of Anacreon, a Greek poet of the sixth century {{sc|b. c.}}, whose odes and epigrams were celebrated for their ease and grace. They were devoted to the praise of love and wine. Hence—'''2.''' Pertaining to the praise of love and wine; convivial; amatory.
{{smaller block|Constantinople had given him a taste for ''Anacreontic'' singing and female society of the questionable kind.
{{right|''R. F. Burton'', El-Medinah, p. 88.}}}}
'''II.''' ''n.'' [''l. c.''] [=F. ''anacréontique''.] A poem by Anacreon, or composed in the manner of Anacreon; a little poem in praise of love and wine. Formerly sometimes written ''anacreontique''.
{{smaller block|To the miscellanies [of Cowley] succeed the ''anacreontiques''.
{{right|''Johnson'', Cowley.}}}}

'''anacrotic''' (an-ạ-krot′ik), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀνά, up, + κρότος, striking, clapping: cf. ἀνακροτεῖν, lift up and clap (the hands).] Displaying or relating to anacrotism.

